::ALG2_U05_L06_GP_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L06_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>Find the inverse of f(x)=x+4. {
= f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=x-4#Correct. Subtract 4 to undo adding 4.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=x+4#This does not undo the original function.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=1/(x+4)#Inverse function does not mean reciprocal.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=4x#This does not undo adding 4.
}

::ALG2_U05_L06_GP_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L06_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>Find the inverse of f(x)=2x. {
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=2x#This repeats the original function.
= f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=x/2#Correct. Divide by 2 to undo multiplying by 2.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=x-2#This undoes adding 2, not multiplying by 2.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=1/(2x)#This is a reciprocal, not inverse function.
}

::ALG2_U05_L06_GP_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L06_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>Find the inverse of f(x)=3x+6. {
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=3x-6#This does not undo both operations.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=(x+6)/3#The sign on 6 is wrong.
= f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=(x-6)/3#Correct. Switch x and y, then solve for y.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=1/(3x+6)#Inverse notation is not reciprocal.
}

::ALG2_U05_L06_GP_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L06_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>Which check verifies inverses? {
~ f(x)+g(x)=x#Addition is not the inverse check.
~ f(x)g(x)=1#That checks reciprocals in special cases, not inverse functions.
~ f(x)=g(x)#Inverse functions usually are not identical.
= f(g(x))=x and g(f(x))=x#Correct. Both compositions must return the original input.
}

::ALG2_U05_L06_GP_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L06_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>What does f<sup>-1</sup>(x) mean? {
= The inverse function of f#Correct. It reverses the input-output relationship of f.
~ The reciprocal of f(x)#That would be 1/f(x).
~ The negative of f(x)#That would be -f(x).
~ The derivative of f#Derivative notation is different.
}

